          <article class="archive-item" id="post-14790">
            <p class="archive-meta"><time datetime="2026-09-24">24 September 2026</time> &middot; on Zenity Labs</p>
            <h3>SalesBleed: Anonymous Phishing via Agentforce in Slack</h3>
            <p>Agentforce agents can be hijacked to send phishing messages in Slack without user confirmation or attribution. This vulnerability stems from the Reply to a Slack Thread action, which allows messages to be sent without any validation. Combined with URL-redaction bypasses, it enables both internal users and external attackers to exploit the agent’s identity for malicious purposes.</p>
            <p>The lack of confirmation and attribution creates a critical gap. An attacker can inject malicious instructions into data, trick the agent into sending phishing links, and obscure the origin of the message. This makes the attack both stealthy and convincing, leveraging the agent’s credibility to bypass normal scrutiny.</p>
            <p>Salesforce addressed the issue by updating defaults and adding proper attribution. However, the risk remains if configurations are not carefully managed. Organizations must ensure write actions require confirmation and that attribution is always visible. This is essential for preventing agentic AI from becoming a vector for phishing.</p>
            <p>The broader lesson is clear: agentic AI integrations with trusted tools like Slack expand an attacker’s reach. Every additional data source or platform connection increases the attack surface. Governance, user confirmation, and visibility are non-negotiable for securing these systems.</p>
            <p class="archive-tags">AIsecurity AgenticAI Phishing ZeroTrust LLMSecurity CyberRisk</p>
          </article>
          <article class="archive-item" id="post-14748">
            <p class="archive-meta"><time datetime="2026-09-24">24 September 2026</time> &middot; on UpGuard</p>
            <h3>Everything Everywhere: Systemic Data Exposure in Supabase Apps | UpGuard</h3>
            <p>The systemic misconfigurations in Supabase are exposing vast amounts of personal data globally. These issues stem from default settings that prioritize ease of use over security. As AI coding agents like Claude Code grow in popularity, they&#x27;re creating thousands of Supabase instances with insecure configurations. This isn&#x27;t new—S3 and GitHub faced similar problems years ago. The lesson is clear: convenience shouldn&#x27;t come at the cost of security.</p>
            <p>The misconfigurations often lack basic access controls. Even when row-level security is enabled, it&#x27;s frequently misapplied or left unconfigured. Vibe coders, typically with limited experience, may not understand how to secure their databases properly. This leads to widespread exposure of PII, authentication tokens, and other sensitive data. The scale is staggering, with thousands of apps leaking information across industries and regions.</p>
            <p>The impact spans B2C and B2B sectors alike. Ecommerce and restaurants are particularly vulnerable, but even professional services and job boards are at risk. The data often includes financial details, user messages, and personal identifiers. These leaks aren&#x27;t isolated incidents—they&#x27;re part of a global trend driven by the rapid adoption of tools like Supabase. The human factor remains the weakest link, especially when AI agents handle the configuration.</p>
            <p>We need stronger governance and security practices in cloud infrastructure. AI-driven development demands new approaches to ensure misconfigurations don&#x27;t lead to systemic data exposures. As leaders in security, we must advocate for better defaults, more education, and proactive monitoring. The time to act is now before the damage becomes irreversible.</p>
            <p class="archive-tags">AIsecurity CloudSecurity DataPrivacy ZeroTrust CyberGovernance SupabaseExposure</p>
          </article>
          <article class="archive-item" id="post-14767">
            <p class="archive-meta"><time datetime="2026-09-13">13 September 2026</time> &middot; on Ctrl Alt Intel</p>
            <h3>Russian and Allied Government Systems Compromised in Targeted Campaigns</h3>
            <p>The exposure of government directories reveals a critical vulnerability in how we secure our infrastructure. When state-aligned actors exploit open directories, they gain access to sensitive systems, credentials, and operational data. The recent compromises of Russian, Kyrgyz, and Syrian government systems highlight the risks of weak access controls and insufficient monitoring. These attacks didn&#x27;t rely on sophisticated malware but on exposed administrative interfaces and default credentials. It&#x27;s a stark reminder that even the most secure environments can be breached through poor governance.</p>
            <p>The Atlas compromise exemplifies this. A roleless account accessed protected administrative data, leading to configuration changes and potential server-side execution. This shows that access controls must be rigorously enforced, and every administrative action must be logged and audited. Organizations need to adopt zero-trust principles and ensure that even internal users operate under strict privilege boundaries. Without these measures, attackers can pivot through the network with minimal friction.</p>
            <p>Supply chain compromises are equally concerning. The Kyrgyz MFA breach demonstrated how a single misconfigured web application can lead to full system compromise. Attackers uploaded a PHP file, executed it as www-data, and deployed a VShell agent for C2. This underscores the need for continuous monitoring of all endpoints and the importance of securing every layer of the infrastructure. We must treat every component as a potential attack surface and implement robust detection and response capabilities.</p>
            <p>The implications are clear: we cannot afford to ignore exposed directories or assume that internal systems are safe. Cybersecurity must be proactive, not reactive. We need to build systems that can detect and respond to threats in real time, while also enforcing strict access controls and continuous monitoring. The future of security lies in agility, visibility, and a relentless focus on protecting the most critical assets.</p>
            <p class="archive-tags">Cybersecurity ZeroTrust SupplyChainSecurity AIsecurity ThreatIntel SecurityOps</p>
          </article>
