            <p>The numbers are telling. CVE disclosures are up sharply across major software suppliers like Chrome, VMware, and Apache. This surge suggests AI is playing a growing role in finding vulnerabilities. But it&#x27;s still early days, and not all increases can be pinned on AI alone.</p>
            <p>The shift is clear. Tools like Anthropic&#x27;s Mythos are being used by companies like Mozilla and Google to identify and fix latent issues. Yet results vary—some find real bugs, others generate noise. The key is to stay focused on what matters: real threats.</p>
            <p>Open source projects are testing grounds for these models. GitHub saw a massive spike in reports, spread across many contributors. It’s a systemic change, not just one tool or person. The ecosystem is evolving, and defenders need to adapt.</p>
            <p>We’re only starting to see the impact. Higher volumes mean more work for security teams. Prioritize threats actively being exploited. Stay ahead by patching early, using threat intel, and embracing tools that help you manage this new reality.</p>
